  "style": {
    "name": "Jade-Lacquer Tide Temple (invented East-Asian/maritime hybrid)",
    "rationale": "An invented shrine of a sea-faring order: vermilion lacquer columns (red concrete) with gold capitals and bracket-stair corbels stand proud of a jade-teal terracotta wall, all on a dark polished-blackstone stylobate with a forward stair. Six columns set an A-B-C-B-A bay rhythm (4-4-7-4-4) around a wide central portal with a stepped stair arch, gold keystone, glazed light-blue tympanum panel and a glowing shroomlight transom over dark-oak doors. Side bays carry recessed glass windows lit from behind by sea lanterns, framed by red-nether-brick wall jambs, projecting stair sills and hoods, with orange glazed medallions above. A dark-prismarine dado and glazed band define the base; a red-nether beam, glazed frieze punctuated by sea lanterns over each column, and an overhanging blackstone stair cornice form the entablature. The crown is three stacked, set-back pagoda eaves of oxidized copper with up-turned tips and hanging lanterns, separated by red terracotta attics (lantern-lit lattice windows, gold-framed sea-lantern oculus), culminating in a ridge and gold finial. Relief runs from the recessed openings at z=-4 through wall z=-3, columns z=-2..-1, to the cornice, eaves and steps at z=0..1."
  },
